                <p class="snippet" >
                    However, a reading of the Court of Appeal’s decision in <b>Jones v. Tsige</b> reveals that both the letter and spirit of the Court’s decision and the policy reasons behind it, prescribe a narrow – do not open the floodgates of liability – ambit for the tort of intrusion on seclusion. &nbsp;&nbsp;[...]&nbsp;&nbsp;As explained in <b>Jones v. Tsige</b>, 2012 ONCA 32, 108 O.R. (3d) 241, at paras. 39-41, informational privacy is concerned primarily with individual autonomy. 
